#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552613} :activity/id #uuid "136bc4b9-49de-551a-beaf-11a5b1fe1f87" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c7d71f-f00f-5dc9-aeb4-885155387bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380945} {:answer/id #uuid "83649048-f61a-5a4f-9f1a-a9bd0f8ca399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{4}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380946} {:answer/id #uuid "ece3b971-05fd-5c98-a0f4-285c95b8ed48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380947} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc644eb-8546-51f1-b7ea-0f19302e599c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{4}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-2}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380948} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d962e7-30e5-58e7-bd21-f0a369727307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380949} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b4ec09-37c7-5b51-8059-90f4778e5356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c17aef4e-54b4-5fd5-b0e4-5e8c5c062f24" :question/math-academy-id "q-132240" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a7add911aa0a7414c979d758cb9a6e9e9d23f8fcfbb71abb1648aeefe30bc6e.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $y=4$ and the curve $y={x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/a5/a5d544a1c4c7d0227e464aae92f8b282f716fa41ee57daa1061e77e1898a8a00.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}{e}^{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1975b0e4-e5be-5e8b-933c-6e8693fb7745" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f0b32b-87d1-52fb-a95a-235990611124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382106} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5589e6-a88a-53c4-aea9-8f6d55058617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382107} {:answer/id #uuid "cea21d1b-e13b-547c-8bfa-fdc7c9f690d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382108} {:answer/id #uuid "22ffdcc1-0c44-522e-980d-fa08f09df48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3-x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382109} {:answer/id #uuid "14ecde84-407f-5be2-9298-26d5db9671e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}{\\int }_{1}^{3-x}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382107} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b20aa2-0826-5294-86a8-f9dba0a906aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d46d3f2d-a87e-594e-ab18-188d9dd35041" :question/math-academy-id "q-40821" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d66848f6030504013f8670ab0c8885b5276c833f2e420151e146fcff823ba6f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=3-x,$ $y=0,$ and $x=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/47/479da73ff2382e584ec7eb02ff2517c7fa21322faf5ecbda2d2ff2d65049ef31.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "38d3c1c5-87bb-5ea8-b4a9-2b91063c1c36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b5c7c3-c26e-582d-bb2e-8f042816f6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348753} {:answer/id #uuid "42d940c0-05b6-554b-bc8c-35dfa257887d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349052} {:answer/id #uuid "84a7742a-fffe-5f11-907a-8c766b4d7f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349657} {:answer/id #uuid "b908f026-083b-5f77-bf2f-152d5cac9615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350085} {:answer/id #uuid "cb714fbc-b873-50d4-b078-4528eaedae9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349657} :answer-field/id #uuid "793a0df5-dacd-550d-b9d0-996f94f43180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3119c509-e446-518c-94fc-dda5b6ec1994" :question/math-academy-id "q-81778" :question/problem "![](images/78/786dd06154d1bda5483ed992dce8922c9629f759b498a3f713c35612311c1ee6.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the line $y=2$ enclosed by the lines $y=x$ and $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the line $y=x$ with the line $y=2.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/92/92b1eda4ccf9e62e1f7310b1ba9bef5cc6c434755e74196d1829a30f2c4a3c53.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{8x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ccab73b5-b3d8-5e48-adf9-78b2cf4731f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baf9f366-76b2-5eb7-ad69-d8a2aebcb5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380966} {:answer/id #uuid "47b43bfc-dd45-5714-bb1a-6f4698dbf38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380967} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd8fa31-c85e-507d-8529-51da72de5124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380968} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3376e3-1c16-5e7b-9367-49d4b7b29c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380969} {:answer/id #uuid "2c994e24-2928-5542-87f6-0ad40b8982fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b05dfc6-3d39-5c0e-b32c-791f40800209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7fd4d0e6-fb9b-5edf-bf95-e5b4e7371cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132283" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{2}-0)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})dx & ={[2x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6}-0 \\\\  & =4-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d1fb68a1-7a13-559e-a41a-df60fc1cda35"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13da6bfc-b076-5886-a5dd-5a3fdd48747d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321307} {:answer/id #uuid "318b0f7e-21e0-5cc8-af2c-d6e582e526f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321527} {:answer/id #uuid "4da2c972-b43d-5c3a-abbb-687bf8c86204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321759} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c566ac-140c-5c66-9504-ef71f8c63c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323053} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d79621-816b-549a-919b-4078e8a77dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9f3ace-9259-58fb-b8f4-56fdfd0930e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65088761-af09-53a6-a000-ed71575458ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-37859" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/x}^{4x}y\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/x}^{4x}y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/x}^{4x}y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}([\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{1/x}^{4x})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2x}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1f09156-dd59-55de-9346-a705c3bd0957" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552550}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551647}]}
